; Comparing an account total that holds several commodities with an
; amount is an error on every run, whichever component comes first.
;
; A balance is below an amount when each of its components is, and
; Assets:Mixed holds $5.00 and 10 EUR: 10 EUR cannot be compared with
; $3.00, so `total < $3` is an error, and so is `total >= $3`, its
; negation.  Ledger used to compare the components in the order in which
; the balance happened to keep them and stop at the first one that settled
; the answer.  That order met 10 EUR first here, so the error was
; reported; had it met $5.00 first, `$5.00 >= $3.00` would have made
; `total < $3` false, and `total >= $3` true, without an error.  That
; order changes from run to run, so every component's commodity is now
; checked before any is compared.
;
; A value expression evaluates `a > b` as `b < a`, so `total > $3`
; compares the amount with the balance, which needs the balance as a
; single amount and fails with another message, as it always did.
;
; The comparisons on this journal that answer are in
; value-compare-mixed-balance.test: the semantic bisimulation skips a
; file with an error block, and still replays that one.

2024/01/01 Opening
    Assets:Mixed    $5.00
    Assets:Mixed    10 EUR
    Equity

2024/01/02 Lunch
    Expenses:Food       $5.00
    Liabilities:Card

test bal Assets -d 'total < $3' -> 1
__ERROR__
While evaluating value expression:
  (total < $3.00)
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Error: Cannot compare amounts with different commodities: 'EUR' and '$'
end test

test bal Assets -d 'total >= $3' -> 1
__ERROR__
While evaluating value expression:
  (total >= $3.00)
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Error: Cannot compare amounts with different commodities: 'EUR' and '$'
end test

test bal Assets -d 'total > $3' -> 1
__ERROR__
While evaluating value expression:
  (total > $3.00)
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
While converting $5.00
10 EUR to an amount:
Error: Cannot convert a balance with multiple commodities to an amount
end test
